        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          When the Mir '97 mission began, the Mir station had been in operation for more than ten years.  One hundred and sixteen scientists from a total of 11 nations (Germany, United States, The Netherlands, Russia, France, Denmark, Belgium, Great Britain, Switzerland, Sweden, Italy and Bulgaria) contributed experiments to this mission.  The cooperation established between the nations and the different scientific disciplines within the Mir '97 mission paved the way for utilization of the International Space Station (ISS).  On November 20, 1996, the freight spacecraft Progress-M36 launched from the Baikonur cosmodrome in Kazakhstan, Russia carrying the scientific payload for the Mir '97 mission.  Nearly three months later, on February 10, 1997, the Russian Soyuz TM 25 lifted off carrying the Mir 23 crewmembers, Vasiliy Tsibliyev (RSA - Russian Space Agency) and Aleksandr Lazutkin (RSA), and the Mir '97 cosmonaut, Reinhold Ewald (DLR - German Space Agency).<P>Upon reaching Mir, German cosmonaut Reinhold Ewald remained on board the station for 20 days with the Mir 22/Mir23/NASA 4 crewmembers.  Within that time, he performed 27 experiments relating to life sciences, materials science, technology and operational tests.  The primary emphasis of the scientific program was on human physiological investigations, including studies on the influence of microgravity on the cardiovascular system, hormonal regulation and metabolism, as well as the muscular and skeletal system.  Material science experiments included investigations into crystal growth on semiconductors and nucleation in glass.  Of the two experiments in the technology field, one related to air analysis on Mir while the other dealt with measuring residual accelerations at various locations in the station.  Operational tests performed on board Mir related to teletraining and teleoperations, which will be of great use for future operations on the ISS.<P>If more than three people were residing on Mir at a time, lithiumperchlorate candles were burned for oxygen generation.  On February 23, a fire, caused by an overheated oxygen cartridge, broke out in the Kvant I module.  However, the crewmembers were not injured.<P>The Mir '97 mission ended on March 2, 1996, as the Soyuz TM 24 capsule landed in the Kazah steppe near Karaganda returning the Mir 22 crew and cosmonaut Ewald.  Ewald spent several weeks following the mission at the cosmonaut training center near Moscow where comprehensive follow-up examinations were conducted.  The resulting data were compared to inflight data to determine the influence of weightlessness on the human body.<P>Because the Mir station offered a unique opportunity for long duration investigations under microgravity conditions, the German mission Mir '97 was extended after Ewald returned.  In Mir '97 E (extension), Russian cosmonauts continued to operate German experiments on the Mir station to enhance the expected scientific results.<END>
        </div>

